---
import DocsLayout from "../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import PageBody from "../components/PageBody.astro";
import Section from "../components/Section.astro";
import Code from "../components/Code.astro";
import TokenTable from "../components/api/TokenTable.astro";
import { defineSections } from "../lib/sections";

const S = defineSections([
  { id: "anatomy", label: "Anatomy", heading: false },
  { id: "total", label: "Total" },
  { id: "gauge", label: "Gauge" },
  { id: "rings", label: "Ring size and tracks" },
  { id: "cards", label: "Chart cards" },
  { id: "data", label: "Values and colors" },
  { id: "accessibility", label: "Accessibility" },
  { id: "javascript", label: "JavaScript" },
  { id: "tokens", label: "Tokens" },
]);

const plans: [string, number][] = [["Free plan", 80], ["Basic plan", 70], ["Premium plan", 60], ["Enterprise plan", 50]];
const visitors: [string, number][] = [["Organic", 80], ["Direct", 75], ["Referral", 70], ["Social", 65]];
---
<DocsLayout page="radial-chart">
<PageBody sections={S}>
  <Section {...S.anatomy}>
        <p class="flow-text">
          A <code>&lt;figure class="radial-chart"&gt;</code> draws a
          <code>&lt;table&gt;</code> of progress values as rings. The first
          column of the table names each row and the second holds its value.
        </p>
        <p>
          It takes the same markup as a <a href="column-chart.html">column
          chart</a>. Each row is a ring, outermost first, filled clockwise from
          the top as a share of 100. A legend lists the rows and their cells
          when there is more than one. A tooltip follows the pointer across
          the rings, and the arrow keys move it when the chart has focus. Keep
          a radial chart to about four rings; to compare more values, use a
          <a href="bar-chart.html">bar chart</a>.
        </p>
        <figure class="radial-chart" data-markdown-ignore>
          <figcaption>Plan goals reached, 2026</figcaption>
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Plan</th><th>Goal reached</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>{plans.map(([plan, value]) => <tr><th>{plan}</th><td>{value}%</td></tr>)}</tbody>
          </table>
        </figure>
<Code code={`
<figure class="radial-chart">
  <figcaption>Plan goals reached, 2026</figcaption>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Plan</th><th>Goal reached</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th>Free plan</th><td>80%</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Basic plan</th><td>70%</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Premium plan</th><td>60%</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Enterprise plan</th><td>50%</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</figure>
`} />
        <p>
          The legend and tooltip show each cell's text as written, so format
          values in the table. For values out of something other than 100,
          set <code>data-max</code> on the figure.
        </p>
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.total}>
        <p>
          A <code>&lt;tfoot&gt;</code> row shows in the middle: its value
          large, with its label below. It is not drawn as a ring. A single
          ring with a total is a progress dial.
        </p>
        <figure class="radial-chart" style="--md-comp-radial-chart-thickness: 14" data-markdown-ignore>
          <figcaption>Monthly sales target</figcaption>
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Target</th><th>Reached</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody><tr><th>Sales</th><td>55%</td></tr></tbody>
            <tfoot><tr><th>Sales</th><td>250</td></tr></tfoot>
          </table>
        </figure>
<Code code={`
<figure class="radial-chart" style="--md-comp-radial-chart-thickness: 14">
  <figcaption>Monthly sales target</figcaption>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Target</th><th>Reached</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th>Sales</th><td>55%</td></tr>
    </tbody>
    <tfoot>
      <tr><th>Sales</th><td>250</td></tr>
    </tfoot>
  </table>
</figure>
`} />
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.gauge}>
        <p>
          <code>gauge</code> draws each ring as a half circle over the middle,
          filling from the left. The total sits on the gauge's base line.
          Here <code>data-max="25"</code> makes each value a share of 25 seats.
        </p>
        <figure class="radial-chart gauge" data-max="25" data-markdown-ignore>
          <figcaption>Team capacity</figcaption>
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Seats</th><th>Count</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><th>Filled</th><td>18</td></tr>
              <tr><th>Offered</th><td>21</td></tr>
            </tbody>
            <tfoot><tr><th>Total seats</th><td>25</td></tr></tfoot>
          </table>
        </figure>
<Code code={`
<figure class="radial-chart gauge" data-max="25">
  <figcaption>Team capacity</figcaption>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Seats</th><th>Count</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th>Filled</th><td>18</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Offered</th><td>21</td></tr>
    </tbody>
    <tfoot>
      <tr><th>Total seats</th><td>25</td></tr>
    </tfoot>
  </table>
</figure>
`} />
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.rings}>
        <p>
          <code>--md-comp-radial-chart-thickness</code> sets a ring's width and
          <code>--md-comp-radial-chart-gap</code> the space between rings, both
          in hundredths of the diameter. When there are too many rings to
          leave a middle, the rings and gaps thin together to fit. Set <code>--md-comp-radial-chart-track-color</code>
          to <code>transparent</code> to hide the tracks behind the rings.
        </p>
        <figure class="radial-chart" style="--md-comp-radial-chart-track-color: transparent; --md-comp-radial-chart-gap: 3" data-markdown-ignore>
          <figcaption>Visitors by source, share of goal</figcaption>
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>Share of goal</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>{visitors.map(([source, value]) => <tr><th>{source}</th><td>{value}%</td></tr>)}</tbody>
          </table>
        </figure>
<Code code={`
<figure class="radial-chart"
  style="--md-comp-radial-chart-track-color: transparent; --md-comp-radial-chart-gap: 3">
  <figcaption>Visitors by source, share of goal</figcaption>
  <table><!-- … --></table>
</figure>
`} />
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.cards}>
        <p>
          A chart goes in a card with the figures it explains. The card adds
          no chart styles; size the rings with
          <code>--md-comp-radial-chart-height</code>, their diameter.
        </p>
        <article class="outlined" data-markdown-ignore>
          <dl class="stats my-0">
            <div><dt>Response rate</dt><dd>72%</dd><dd class="stat-change up">+14 points from last month</dd></div>
          </dl>
          <figure class="radial-chart" style="--md-comp-radial-chart-height: 180px">
            <figcaption class="visually-hidden">Response rate by month</figcaption>
            <table>
              <thead><tr><th>Month</th><th>Response rate</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody><tr><th>This month</th><td>72%</td></tr><tr><th>Last month</th><td>58%</td></tr></tbody>
              <tfoot><tr><th>This month</th><td>72%</td></tr></tfoot>
            </table>
          </figure>
        </article>
<Code code={`
<article class="outlined">
  <dl class="stats my-0">
    <div>
      <dt>Response rate</dt>
      <dd>72%</dd>
      <dd class="stat-change up">+14 points from last month</dd>
    </div>
  </dl>
  <figure class="radial-chart" style="--md-comp-radial-chart-height: 180px">
    <figcaption class="visually-hidden">Response rate by month</figcaption>
    <table><!-- … --></table>
  </figure>
</article>
`} />
        <p>
          <code>sparkline</code> is a 48px chart with no legend or tooltip, for
          a small dial or gauge beside a figure that already explains it.
        </p>
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.data}>
        <p>
          Cells read as in a column chart: a cell's number is its
          <code>data-value</code>, or its text without currency signs, percent
          signs or grouping commas. A ring's fill is its value's share of
          <code>data-max</code>, 100 by default; a maximum of zero or less is
          ignored. Values past the maximum close the ring. A value under
          about one ring's width of arc draws as a dot at the start, and zero,
          negative and missing values leave only the track. Their rows stay in
          the legend and the tooltip. A chart whose cells are all gaps shows
          its table.
        </p>
        <p>
          Rings take <code>primary</code>, <code>tertiary</code>,
          <code>secondary</code> and <code>on-surface-variant</code> in turn;
          a fifth ring repeats the first color.
        </p>
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.accessibility}>
        <p>
          The table is the chart's text alternative. Give it a header row and
          a header cell per row, and name the chart with a
          <code>&lt;figcaption&gt;</code>. The drawn rings, total and legend
          are hidden from assistive technology.
        </p>
        <p>
          The plot is one Tab stop named by the caption. Down and right move
          to the next ring inwards, up and left to the one before, wrapping
          between the innermost ring and the outermost. Home and End jump to
          the ends and Escape hides the tooltip. On a touch screen, tap a ring
          to show it. In a right-to-left page the rings and the arrows still
          run clockwise. The tooltip is a
          polite live region, so a screen reader hears each row's values as it
          moves. A sparkline is not focusable. In forced colors the rings keep
          their colors so the legend still keys them.
        </p>
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.javascript}>
        <p>
          <code>AutoInit()</code> starts every <code>.radial-chart</code>. To
          start one yourself, or to redraw a chart after changing its table or
          its ring tokens, call <code>RadialChart.init</code> again. It has the
          line chart's methods; the <code>max</code> option sets the maximum
          when the figure has no <code>data-max</code>, and <code>min</code>
          does not apply.
        </p>
<Code code={`
const chart = Expressive.RadialChart.init(document.querySelector('.radial-chart'));
chart.show(1);   // highlight the second ring; -1 hides the tooltip
chart.destroy(); // remove the drawing and show the table again
`} />
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.tokens}>
        <p>Override these on the <code>.radial-chart</code>.</p>
        <TokenTable>
          <tr><td><code>--md-comp-radial-chart-height</code></td><td>240px, the rings' diameter; 48px for a sparkline</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>--md-comp-radial-chart-thickness</code></td><td>8, a ring's width in hundredths of the diameter</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>--md-comp-radial-chart-gap</code></td><td>2, the gap between rings in the same units</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>--md-comp-radial-chart-track-color</code></td><td><code>surface-container-highest</code>; <code>transparent</code> hides the tracks</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>--md-comp-radial-chart-color-1</code> … <code>-4</code></td><td><code>primary</code>, <code>tertiary</code>, <code>secondary</code>, <code>on-surface-variant</code></td></tr>
        </TokenTable>
        <p>For a selective Sass build, include <code>"radial-chart"</code> in <code>$components</code>.</p>
  </Section>
</PageBody>
</DocsLayout>
